        callouts:[
          {role:"student", type:"exam", text:"Codeine CYP2D6 pharmacogenomics: ultra-rapid metabolisers convert codeine to morphine faster and more completely than normal → dangerously high morphine levels from standard doses. This is why codeine is CONTRAINDICATED in children <12 years and in breastfeeding (infant morphine exposure via breast milk). The same mechanism explains inter-individual variation in codeine efficacy — poor metabolisers get no antitussive effect."},
          {role:"doctor", type:"clinical", text:"Refractory chronic cough — 'cough hypersensitivity syndrome' — is increasingly recognised. Once common causes are excluded and treated, emerging options include low-dose morphine (evidence-based — Morice et al.), speech therapy/cough suppression techniques, and P2X3 antagonists (gefapixant — approved in some jurisdictions for chronic refractory cough; reduces cough by blocking ATP-stimulated C-fibres)."},
          {role:"pharmacist", type:"clinical", text:"OTC cough preparations in Ghana frequently contain codeine, chlorpheniramine, and pseudoephedrine in combination. Do NOT dispense codeine-containing products to patients <12 years or their caregivers. Ask about concurrent SSRIs/tramadol (serotonin risk with DXM/codeine), other CNS depressants, and breastfeeding. Dextromethorphan-based products are a safer alternative for most patients."},
          {role:"nurse", type:"clinical", text:"A PRODUCTIVE cough in a patient with pneumonia, COPD exacerbation, or post-operative atelectasis should NOT receive an antitussive — the cough is doing necessary work. Priority: chest physiotherapy, upright positioning, adequate hydration, supported coughing technique. Antitussive prescriptions in patients with productive cough should be queried with the prescribing team."},
          {role:"labtech", type:"practical", text:"For a meaningful sputum culture: early morning deep-cough specimen; reject if >25 squamous epithelial cells/low power field (saliva contamination). For TB: minimum two specimens on consecutive days; GeneXpert MTB/RIF is the preferred initial test. Antitussives given before sputum collection may suppress the cough needed for an adequate specimen — liaise with clinical team on timing."},
        ]
